# Form: {{ story_type }}
The story is read straight through, first page to last. Never offer the reader choices or refer to page numbers.
{% if story_type == "picture-book" %}Exactly {{ num_pages }} pages, 1–3 short sentences per page. Simple vivid words. One clear visual moment per page.{% elif story_type == "short-story" %}About {{ num_pages }} pages; each page is 1–3 short paragraphs. A complete arc: setup, tension, resolution.{% elif story_type == "fable" %}About {{ num_pages }} pages, 2–5 sentences each. Animal (or object) characters embodying the ideas in the sources. End with a one-sentence "moral" field distilling the lesson.{% elif story_type == "bedtime" %}Exactly {{ num_pages }} pages, 1–3 gentle sentences each. A soothing arc that grows calmer page by page; the last page is quiet and sleepy. No peril.{% endif %}

# Cast
Give every character an ordinary human first name — Maya, Theo, Amara, Ines, Samuel — never a descriptive label ("the Curious One"), a title, or a species name.{% if story_type == "fable" %} The animal characters get human first names too.{% endif %}

Each character's appearance is fixed for the entire book. Write it once, in "visual", as a spec an illustrator repeats verbatim on every page:
- For a person, state in this order: apparent age, **skin tone as a named color** (deep brown, warm olive, pale freckled, golden tan), **hair color and style** (tight black coils, straight auburn bob), eye color, build, clothing with named colors, one signature accessory.
- For an animal, state: species, fur/feather/scale colors, distinguishing markings, one signature accessory.
Never leave skin tone or hair color unstated, and never change a character's skin, hair, clothing, or accessories between pages — not in "visual", not in "scene", not in the page text.

# Nothing is written down
The illustrator cannot draw legible words, so the story must never depend on any. Do not write a moment in which a character reads, writes, points at, or discusses words on a whiteboard, chalkboard, screen, sign, poster, or page — those scenes force garbled lettering into the picture. Say it aloud in dialogue or show it through action instead. No "scene" may call for signs, labels, banners, posters, titles, book covers, letters, or numbers.

